Z||000000||MontanaFiero||02-25-2001||02:28 PM||||Thought you diehards would enjoy seeing some pictures of my first engine removal and the method I chose to use.  It was much easier than I expected. I did this alone and in a car port when it finally "warmed up" to about 35 degrees.<P>Now to get the damned thing rebuilt. I am not going to spend a lot of $$$, but I do plan on attempting some port matching. Any thoughts?<P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1518249&a=11475761&p=41979868"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1518249&a=11475761&p=41979867"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://albums.photopoint.com/j/View?u=1518249&a=11475761&p=41979864"> <P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;||||reg||6
Z||000001||2xsess||02-25-2001||06:01 PM||||Cool, I haven't done it yet. almost want to after hearing everyone say how easy it is.<BR><P>------------------<BR>Jeff<BR>AOL IM : Only2xsess<BR>       <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/2xsess.gif">      <BR> <A HREF="http://kresgeus.homestead.com/FieroPage.html" TARGET=_blank>Visit My Website</A>||||reg||1
Z||000002||My7Fieros||02-25-2001||08:06 PM||||How long did it take you? What are some pointers you can give...any "trouble spots"...I'd really like to know...Ive got to do it this summer. Thanx<P>------------------<BR> <IMG SRC="http://tgowens.com/My7Fieros_sig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000003||JohnnyK||02-25-2001||08:29 PM||||Well, if you live in a rusty zone like I do, the two back bolts that hold the cradle up have two nuts that are welded inside the frame. Well, they rusted loose, so I had to cut a hole in the frame to get a grip on it. I've heard this is quite common.||||reg||1
Z||000004||bHooper||02-25-2001||09:59 PM||||hey!  that looks familiar!<P>ahh, the many wonderfull use's of a palette... don't forget to change the plugs while she's out, and look the manifolds over real good!<P>hoop||||reg||1
Z||000005||sdm1964||02-25-2001||10:06 PM||||2xsess, In a few months after we get back you can help me put the motor back in!!! I used the same princible but a 10 ton floor jack and a piece of 4x4. It has taken me longer to get the stuff done that I thought. It is on hold for now.Good luck on yours.||||reg||14
Z||000006||MontanaFiero||02-26-2001||01:02 AM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by My7Fieros:<BR><B>How long did it take you? What are some pointers you can give...any "trouble spots"...I'd really like to know...Ive got to do it this summer. Thanx<P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P><I>Gee-- where should I start</I>...<BR> First, if you have air conditioning tie it up with a coat hanger and (guessing of course) take all the bolts off that hold the upper bracket to the block. (putting this back ought to be <I>very</I> interesting!)<P> Second, there is a second wiring harness that goes up to a block next to the battery. remove the screw (1/4") and then you will have to take it apart. (Don't do what I did and jack the car up and use this harness to support the motor with.)<P> <B>Anyone seen a 15" version of these wheels? </B>(above, on the car)<P>------------------<BR>&gt;&gt; '87 Coupe 4 cyl, auto &lt;&lt;<BR>&gt;&gt; '86 SE V6, 4 speed- <I>project</I> &lt;&lt;||||reg||1
Z||000007||fierodave||02-26-2001||06:13 AM||||Yep....been there done that...lol  did about 8 of them in the last two weeks, We have it down to 1 hour and 15 minutes, out and on the floor.  first one took almost two hours  grrrr  it gets easy after the 7th time, but i guess I am sort of blessed working on rust free Ca, stuff :~) not one busted or rusted bolt in the past 10 cars! lol <BR>FD<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://members.theglobe.com/fierodave/gifs/fierosig.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||rforbes||02-26-2001||12:59 PM||||Looks like fun, I should know, I am in the process of doing the same thing.  I recently bought a '84 with a hole in one piston.  I had it bored .030, and put all new parts in, plus went through the auto tranny while it was out.  Saturday evening the cradle went back up and got enough bolts to hold it in place when I quit for the night.  I hope to complete the job this week and have it on the road.  This is my firt fiero although I almost bought one about 15 years ago then backed out.  Good thing too, it was to be my only car and two weeks after turning it down we found out the wife was pregnant. <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">  Now 15 years later my son is 14 and looking forward to learning to drive in this fiero!!!||||reg||12
Z||000009||mshill||02-26-2001||01:35 PM||||fierodave,  Yes indeed, rust free california cars are the way to go.  I have three and the only rust on any of them is on the battery tray.  The last one (86GT F50 Project car) seemed like it was way too easy to remove the cradle.||||reg||1
Z||000010||Kevin-84SE||02-26-2001||08:39 PM||||MontanaFiero, <P>   I have seen a set of those wheels in 15", there is a set on my wifes 1987 300zx out in the driveway. <P>   Kevin ||||reg||1
